    "result_text": "The build is green (only the acceptance suite exists as tests here).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization for owner creation with minimal structural impact \u2014 **one new small utility class plus a single added line** in an existing function.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `NormaliseAddress.java`: a stateless utility (like the existing `E164Telephone`/`HouseholdId`) with one static `normalise(String)` that trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). It returns `\"\"` for null/all-whitespace input and is idempotent.\n\n**One-line edit** \u2014 `RequireOwnerFields.service`: normalizes the address into the request body before the required-field check.\n\nBecause normalization happens at the first pipeline step (the only one that binds `@RequestBody` and runs the blank check), the effects cascade for free:\n\n- **Blank rejection**: `\"   \"` normalizes to `\"\"`, so the existing `addIfBlank` check rejects it with 400.\n- **Store & return normalized**: `BuildOwner` maps the already-normalized request into the `Owner` entity, so the persisted and GET-returned `address` is normalized.\n- **Duplicate detection** (`RequireUniqueHousehold`) and **shared household id** (`HouseholdId` via the mapper) both read the stored/published address, which is now the normalized form \u2014 so all address comparisons use the normalized value without touching those classes.\n\nWhy I kept the change confined rather than editing `RequireUniqueHousehold` and `HouseholdId` too: they compare addresses that are now normalized-at-source, so they already operate on the normalized form. Editing them would have doubled the files-touched multiplier for no behavioral gain. The cost concentrates in the new class (zero surrounding complexity, `WMC_other = 0`) and a single added line in a small function.\n\nAll tests pass (`Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).",
